  <Type>
    <Name>SVG.Attribute.accumulate</Name>
    <Members>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>AppliesTo</Name>
        <Value>animate animateMotion animateTransform</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>Description</Name>
        <Value>

{{SVGRef}}

The **`accumulate`** attribute controls whether or not an animation is cumulative.

It is frequently useful for repeated animations to build upon the previous results, accumulating with each iteration. This attribute said to the animation if the value is added to the previous animated attribute's value on each iteration.

You can use this attribute with the following SVG elements:

- [animate](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/web/svg/element/animate)
- [animateMotion](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/web/svg/element/animateMotion)
- [animateTransform](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/web/svg/element/animateTransform)
</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>Header</Name>
        <Value>System.Collections.Hashtable</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>IsDeprecated</Name>
        <Value>False</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>Name</Name>
        <Value>accumulate</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>PSTypeName</Name>
        <Value>SVG.Attribute</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
    </Members>
  </Type>
  <Type>
    <Name>SVG.Attribute.points</Name>
    <Members>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>AppliesTo</Name>
        <Value>polyline polygon polyline polygon</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>Description</Name>
        <Value>

{{SVGRef}}

The **`points`** attribute defines a list of points. Each point is defined by a pair of number representing a X and a Y coordinate in the user coordinate system. If the attribute contains an odd number of coordinates, the last one will be ignored.

You can use this attribute with the following SVG elements:

- [polyline](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/web/svg/element/polyline)
- [polygon](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/web/svg/element/polygon)
</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
      <NoteProperty>
        <Name>Example</Name>
        <Value>```css hidden
html,
body,
svg {
  height: 100%;
}
```
```html
&lt;svg viewBox="-10 -10 220 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"&gt;
  &lt;!-- polyline is an open shape --&gt;
  &lt;polyline stroke="black" fill="none" points="50,0 21,90 98,35 2,35 79,90" /&gt;

  &lt;!-- polygon is a closed shape --&gt;
  &lt;polygon
    stroke="black"
    fill="none"
    transform="translate(100,0)"
    points="50,0 21,90 98,35 2,35 79,90" /&gt;

  &lt;!--
  It is usually considered best practices to separate a X and Y
  coordinate with a comma and a group of coordinates by a space.
  It makes things more readable for human beings.
  --&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;
```</Value>
      </NoteProperty>
      <NoteProperty>
